  {
   "id": "8KyGXT5KBgAJ",
   "from": "Murray Efford",
   "author": "118cea91a1619106",
   "date": "2017-05-25T01:37:34Z",
   "subject": "Re: detection probability of each individual",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Cristiano<br>It would help if you spelled out a little more what you mean. Under the SECR model, probability of detection is a function of location. The function pdot in &#39;secr&#39; gives the probability an individual located at particular coordinates x,y is detected at least once during sampling. Were you wanting something else? The average across an area depends entirely on the extent of the area, which is not really useful.<br>Murray</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>On Thursday, May 25, 2017 at 12:11:50 AM UTC+12, cttrinca wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div style=\"color:#000;background-color:#fff;font-family:Helvetica Neue,Helvetica,Arial,Lucida Grande,sans-serif;font-size:13px\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Group,<br></div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br></div><div dir=\"ltr\">does anyone know how to estimate the detection probability of each individual?</div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br></div><div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks,</div><div dir=\"ltr\"><br></div><div dir=\"ltr\">Cristiano<br></div></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "E3PaS-JSBQAJ",
   "from": "Cristiano Trinca",
   "author": "4140d98df209a83d",
